---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const share = assertion('one-job-in-five-is-nobodys-payroll')
const lost = assertion('every-job-lost-since-2001-is-a-payroll-job')
const farm = assertion('the-farm-proprietors-halved')

const spec = (a: typeof share, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const shareSpec = spec(share, 'Per cent of all employment')
const lostSpec = spec(lost, 'Jobs, 2001 to 2022')
const farmSpec = spec(farm, 'Farm proprietors')
---

<Article slug="nobodys-payroll">
  <p>
    Three federal programmes count the jobs in Allen County and they do not agree. The insurance
    filings find 49,136 covered jobs in 2022. The occupational survey finds 49,640 in 2024. A third
    finds 64,238. The gap is about fourteen thousand jobs, it is not an error, and most of it has a
    name: people working for themselves.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={share} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="share"
    title="Proprietors as a share of all employment in Allen County"
    caption="The 1969–2000 and 2001–2022 figures come from two tables on two industry classifications and are two series, not one; the join moves total employment by 3,148 jobs. Nothing here is differenced across it."
    columns={['Year', 'Proprietor share']}
    rows={share.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="share" set:html={JSON.stringify(shareSpec)} is:inline />
  <Gloss>
    These five line codes have been in this catalog since August and one of them was read. Line 10
    is the total that has appeared on this site for months; lines 20, 40, 50 and 60 split it into
    payroll work, proprietors, farm proprietors and nonfarm proprietors, and had never been opened.
  </Gloss>
  <p>
    The arithmetic closes about as well as arithmetic across three programmes ever does. Take the
    64,238 and remove the 11,964 proprietors and 52,274 payroll jobs are left; the insurance
    filings count 49,136 of those, and the remaining three thousand are jobs that exist but sit
    outside unemployment insurance — some farm work, some of the smallest nonprofits, railroads,
    a few owners of their own incorporated businesses. None of the three counts is wrong. They
    are answers to three different questions, and only one of them is <em>how many people here
    are working</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    An unemployment insurance filing is made by an employer about an employee. An occupational
    survey asks establishments what they pay people to do. Neither instrument has anywhere to put a
    woman who cuts hair in her own chair, a man who hauls with his own truck, or a farmer. So the
    county has a fifth of its working life that two of its three job counts are structurally unable
    to see.
  </p>

  <h2>Everything lost since 2001 was a payroll job</h2>
  <p>
    For thirty-one years the two halves of the county's workforce grew together. Between 1969 and
    2000 payroll employment rose 26.3 per cent and proprietors 48.9. Then they parted, and they have
    been moving in opposite directions ever since.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={lost} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="lost"
    title="What happened to Allen County employment, 2001 to 2022"
    caption="Jobs. Payroll employment fell 13.4 per cent over the period and proprietors rose 26.1, so the county's net loss is about two thirds of its gross one."
    columns={['Component', 'Jobs']}
    rows={lost.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="lost" set:html={JSON.stringify(lostSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The sharpest movement in the whole fifty-four years is the most recent one. Proprietors went
    from 9,946 in 2019 to 11,964 in 2022 — up 2,018, or a fifth, in three years, with nothing like
    it anywhere else in the record. The nearest thing is 1982, the worst year of this county's
    manufacturing collapse, when manufacturing employment stood at 14,349 against a 1973 peak of
    18,400 and the proprietor share hit its high for that whole era.
  </p>
  <p>
    Self-employment rising when payroll work is scarce is a shape both episodes have, and this site
    is not going further than that. Employment counts jobs, not hours and not income. A full-time
    trade and a Saturday one count alike, and nothing here says which the county gained.
  </p>

  <h2>Except on the farms</h2>
  <p>
    One current runs the other way for the whole period. In 1969 the county had 1,668 farm
    proprietors. It has 802.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={farm} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="farm"
    title="Farm proprietors in Allen County, 1969 to 2022"
    caption="The series falls inside the 1969–2000 table, inside the 2001–2022 table, and across the join between them — a trend that survives a change in how everything around it is classified."
    columns={['Year', 'Farm proprietors']}
    rows={farm.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="farm" set:html={JSON.stringify(farmSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Every net proprietor this county has gained since 1969 is a nonfarm one, and then some: the
    nonfarm figure more than doubled while the farm figure halved. The land did not empty — two
    thirds of the county is still cropland and pasture — but the number of people who own the work
    on it has fallen by half in two generations.
  </p>
  <p>
    That number also settles a puzzle left standing elsewhere on this site. The occupational survey
    finds sixty people in farming, fishing and forestry occupations in a county two thirds covered
    by farms, and says so with a shrug: it counts no self-employed person. Here are the ones it
    cannot see — eight hundred and two of them, and 946 farm jobs of every kind.
  </p>
</Article>
